---
import BaseHead from '../../../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../../../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../../../components/Header.astro';

const pageTitle = 'Share the Ride Backend';
const pageDescription = 'Portfolio snapshot of the Share the Ride carpooling platform backend.';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} />
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main class="page">
			<section class="hero">
				<p class="eyebrow">Project Spotlight</p>
				<h1>{pageTitle}</h1>
				<p class="lead">
					Share is the production-ready Node.js and Express service behind the “Share the Ride” carpooling
					platform, delivering secure REST and WebSocket experiences for riders and drivers.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section class="content">
				<h2>What I Built</h2>
				<p>
					I designed and implemented a layered backend that keeps the platform reliable and responsive.
					It orchestrates authentication, ride workflows, chat, notifications, and scheduled maintenance jobs
					while staying adaptable to feature requests from product and mobile teams.
				</p>

				<h3>Highlights</h3>
				<ul>
					<li>JWT-secured APIs covering posts, interests, reviews, and rider onboarding.</li>
					<li>Real-time Socket.IO messaging with optional AES encryption and Firebase fallback alerts.</li>
					<li>Daily jobs that archive stale posts, refresh ride statistics, and prune expired conversations.</li>
				</ul>

				<h3>Architecture Glimpse</h3>
				<p>
					The service follows a clear separation of concerns: Express routers feed thin controllers, business
					logic lives in domain services, and Sequelize modules handle data access for MySQL. Shared utilities
					cover localization, notifications, and encryption so each feature team has consistent building blocks.
				</p>

				<h3>Stack at a Glance</h3>
				<div class="stack-grid">
					<div>
						<h4>Core</h4>
						<p>Node.js, Express 4, Socket.IO, Sequelize, MySQL</p>
					</div>
					<div>
						<h4>Messaging & Auth</h4>
						<p>JWT, Firebase Cloud Messaging, Redis (optional), Nodemailer</p>
					</div>
					<div>
						<h4>Operational</h4>
						<p>node-schedule jobs, Docker-ready configuration, REST client collections</p>
					</div>
				</div>

				<h3>Outcome</h3>
				<p>
					Share has supported multi-language trip planning across web and mobile clients since launch. The
					backend scales comfortably on Node 16+, keeps ride data fresh with automated pruning, and provides a
					single source of truth for notifications and chat history.
				</p>

				<p class="cta">
					Interested in the technical deep dive or API coverage? Let’s chat about the architecture decisions
					and how they can translate to your next mobility project.
				</p>
			</section>
		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	.page {
		margin: 0 auto;
		max-width: 960px;
		padding: 3rem 1.5rem 4rem;
	}

	.hero {
		margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
	}

	.eyebrow {
		color: var(--accent, #4b63ff);
		font-size: 0.9rem;
		font-weight: 600;
		letter-spacing: 0.12em;
		text-transform: uppercase;
	}

	.lead {
		font-size: 1.1rem;
		line-height: 1.7;
		margin-top: 1rem;
		max-width: 48ch;
	}

	.content h2,
	.content h3,
	.content h4 {
		margin-top: 2rem;
	}

	.content ul {
		margin: 1rem 0 1.5rem;
		padding-left: 1.2rem;
	}

	.stack-grid {
		display: grid;
		gap: 1.5rem;
		margin-top: 1rem;
	}

	@media (min-width: 720px) {
		.stack-grid {
			grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
		}
	}

	.stack-grid h4 {
		margin-bottom: 0.4rem;
	}

	.cta {
		border-left: 3px solid var(--accent, #4b63ff);
		margin-top: 2.5rem;
		padding-left: 1rem;
	}
</style>
